\ul{
\li{\strong{The geometric mean goes down} (0.987; CPU 0.994), but two corpora are over the limit in these three rounds: cedar 1.042 and reachability 1.039.}
\li{\strong{Six more rounds of each} (cedar 8 passes, reachability 12 passes per process), nine rounds in all: cedar 1.465 to 1.479 s, ratio 1.010, and its CPU ratio is 1.013 with every one of the nine rounds at or above 1.000 (1.015, 1.026, 1.008, 1.007, 1.009, 1.008, 1.028, 1.000, 1.017); reachability 0.378 to 0.392 s, ratio 1.036 (per round 0.991, 1.077, 1.053, 1.058, 1.111, 1.026, 0.950, 1.025, 1.038), CPU 1.019. A pass of 0.38 s moves by 5 \% between processes, but nine rounds say the same: reachability is over 3 \%.}
\li{\strong{A cause is visible}: C2's inlining log (section 3.2). The split methods are calls on the JVM; the miss of the level cache is the one that runs.}
\li{\strong{So patch 2 fails the guard on the JVM launcher}, by one corpus, and the cost it shows on cedar is small and real. The other seven corpora (cedar with its nine rounds: 1.010) and the geometric mean are inside the rule.}
\li{Peak resident memory of the JVM launcher varies by process under its heap limit of 7 GiB (Mathlib: 6,191 MiB for the base and 7,401 MiB for the candidate as the largest of three rounds; con-leche 2,067 and 1,885 MiB): no trend.}
}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{6. The gates}

\subtree{
\title{6.1 The list (patch 1)}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Nothing changes in the jars.} The fourteen RLVM jars of the build with patch 1 are byte for byte the base's, so the suites of the modules, the gate through the jars, the machine's own gate and the JVM launcher's guard are those of the base.}
\li{\strong{The gate of the default configuration through the executable} (\code{\verb~|scripts/gate.sh --dist .. --defaults --corpus all --hostile --native~}): 230 of 230 published tests correct, none declined; 17,775 mutants with SOUNDNESS 0 and COMPLETENESS 0 (agree 15,739, declined 2,034, two timeouts); the differential self-test caught; hostile exports 1,196 rows: agree 1,124, 68 tolerated accepts of kind (d), 0 wrong accept, 0 wrong reject, 4 declined. PASS.}
\li{\strong{The two timeouts are the host's.} They are \code{\verb~|bugs/proj-of-subst-prop~} and \code{\verb~|bugs/rec-of-subst-prop~} with a changed literal, the two heaviest mutants: 18 to 30 s of CPU each. Run alone they are rejected by the base and by the executable with the list, as by the official checker (exit status 1 four times of four; 24 to 78 s of wall clock on the loaded host). Under the gate's 60 s limit on the efficiency cores they did not finish; the same one or two mutants timed out in every gate of the machine as default engine that ran on the host between 06:04 and 07:40 UTC (six runs, three of them of other distributions), and passed in 22 to 39 s in the release's gates. The step was run again for the corpus of the two when the host had calmed down (load average 9): 2,692 mutants, SOUNDNESS 0, COMPLETENESS 0, agree 2,590, declined 102, no timeout. With it the executable with the list has the release's counts: agree 15,741, declined 2,034.}
\li{\strong{The eight corpora} are accepted through the executable with the list with 4 and with 8 workers (and through the JVM launcher of the same jars).}
\li{\strong{Cost only.} Every corpus with one worker through the base's executable and the one with the list: all counters of the machine and the verdict counters of the driver and of the reference engine are identical on the eight corpora (141 to 146 keys per corpus; the figures are those of the table in section 6.2).}
}
}

