}
}

\subtree{
\title{7. What limits the rest}

\p{With the list the executable executes 71.7 G instructions for an Init pass with one worker (71.4 G with the splits as well) and the JVM launcher 57.3 G: 1.25 times, where it was 1.38. The 3 \% of samples that are still on frame code belong to calls of large functions, which are calls on the JVM too. What remains is how the Community Edition compiler translates the machine's one data structure, an \code{\verb~|int[]~} slab read through a field of the machine (the candidate's disassembly, aarch64):}

\ul{
\li{\strong{A bounds check per element.} A probe compares four words of a record, \code{\verb~|v[o + 1]~} to \code{\verb~|v[o + 3]~} and \code{\verb~|v[o]~}: four compares with the length and four branches. Writing a record is eight stores and eight checks (\code{\verb~|Machine.addRecordAt~} inlined in \code{\verb~|Values.add~}: about 70 instructions for the 8 stores). C2 merges the checks of constant offsets from one index into one or two; this compiler does not, and Java has no way to say it.}
\li{\strong{The mask is not understood.} \code{\verb~|tab[hash & (tab.length - 1)]~} is checked against the length in every step of every probe loop (\code{\verb~|Values.neu~}, \code{\verb~|closureProbe~}, \code{\verb~|rec~}, \code{\verb~|Runs.pruneNear~}), although the source is written that way for the compiler's sake.}
\li{\strong{Three instructions per address.} An element is loaded as: the constant 8 into a register, add the shifted index, load with two registers. Where C2 uses one add and a load with a displacement.}
\li{\strong{Four instructions per array field.} \code{\verb~|m.vh~} is a 32-bit load, a test for null, a zero extension and an add of the heap base, again after every call that is not inlined.}
\li{\strong{A safepoint poll per probe step.} The probe loops are not counted loops, so every iteration loads the thread's poll word and tests it: two of the 15 instructions of a step of \code{\verb~|Values.neu~}.}
\li{\strong{Moves and spills}: 11 \% of the candidate's samples sit on register moves and constants and 7 \% on loads and stores of the stack frame (15 and 8 \% in the base, navier-stokes-euler); all registers are saved by the caller, and the merged functions are large.}
}

\p{None of these is a property of a method that a restructuring could repair; they are per access. A source can only have fewer accesses: two \code{\verb~|int~} words per \code{\verb~|long~} slot, a record's memo words next to its key, arguments of a neutral application inline. That is the layout of the session tables, which is lever 3 of rep-P11, and there it is not cost only. Two observations for that lever, measured here and not acted on: (1) writing the probe loops as counted loops would remove the poll from every step without changing the probe sequence; (2) the hottest instructions of the image are the first loads of a table slot, so the instruction count of a probe is not what a layout change should aim at.}

\p{The other known way to the same instructions is a profile, which only Oracle GraalVM can use (rep-P11, section 4.3).}
}

\subtree{
\title{8. What was not measured}

\ul{
\li{Anything on x86-64, the arena's architecture. The call overhead is the same kind of code there; the three-instruction address is specific to aarch64.}
\li{The guard on a quiet host. Every time here was taken while the other twelve cores were busy; the ratios are from interleaved rounds, the absolute times are not those of the benchmark tables.}
\li{The cycles of the executable with the list alone with one worker inside a window (its instruction count is exact; with the splits as well the cycles are 0.956 of the base's, three rounds).}
\li{The instruction count of the JVM launcher on any corpus but Init, and its cycles inside a window.}
\li{The loader thread and the environment thread separately: the list names eight readers of the term store that they call too; a pass's CPU contains them.}
\li{Each list entry by itself. The list was built in four steps of 15, 27, 14 and 23 entries; two groups were taken out again by measurement (the recursive pair, the two entries of the level store).}
\li{The splits without the one of \code{\verb~|Levels~}, and each split alone on the JVM launcher: the guard was run on the three together.}
\li{The optional Oracle GraalVM build with the list (no Oracle GraalVM was used).}
\li{\strong{An executable without compressed references} was built once (\code{\verb~|-H:-UseCompressedReferences~} on the image with both patches) because an array field then loads in one instruction less: 3.1 \% fewer instructions than with compressed references (138.370 against 142.756 G, Init, one worker), cycles 0.941 against 0.956 of the base in one window of three rounds (not resolved), 5.4 MB more image. It is a flag of the build, not of the machine; its memory with 8 workers and its guard were not measured.}
\li{A clean run of the mutant step for the build with both patches: the heaviest mutant hit the gate's time limit on the loaded host in both of its gates (section 6.2); the step was repeated only for the build with the list.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{9. The patches, and what an integrator must know}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Patch 1, the list (GO)}: a new file \src{rlvm-machine/h10/force-inline.txt} and 31 changed lines of \src{rlvm-machine/h10/native-image-args.sh}. No class file changes. \src{scripts/build.sh} needs no change; the line "inline lists" of a build's record then says 269 forced and 81 never. It applies to the main tree of 2026-11-25 without a failed hunk.}
\li{\strong{Patch 2, the splits (NO-GO as it is)}: \code{\verb~|Machine.java~} (three methods become six), \code{\verb~|Runs.java~} (\code{\verb~|get~}, \code{\verb~|getSlow~}), \code{\verb~|Levels.java~} (\code{\verb~|lsub~}, \code{\verb~|lsubList~} and their two miss methods). Kept as the record of what was measured. It is independent of patch 1 and was not built alone. What would make it a candidate again: without the hunks of \code{\verb~|Levels.java~} (the other two splits move code that a warm pass never runs), built and gated anew; its gain in the image is under half a percent of the instructions.}
\li{\strong{The list names methods of \code{rlvm-store}.} If the owners of the loader and the store rename \code{\verb~|Terms.a~}, \code{\verb~|b~}, \code{\verb~|kind~}, \code{\verb~|fvMask~}, \code{\verb~|hasLevelParam~}, \code{\verb~|ProofBlocks.rd~}, \code{\verb~|localRef~} or \code{\verb~|DeclTable.ctorInduct~}, the entry has to follow; the build does not notice. The other agents of the wave were told.}
\li{\strong{A new hot helper needs an entry, not a split.} A profile of the executable shows it: a small function with samples on its first six instructions. A split helps the image and can cost the JVM launcher a call (section 5.3); the list cannot. The tools of this report (the cost of being called per function, the instruction-level view) are outside the repository; the method is section 1.2.}
\li{The executable must be gated after the list changes; the jars need not be, they do not change.}
\li{\strong{If the level store changes} (another lever of the wave works there): the list forces \code{\verb~|Levels.lsubList~} and with it the miss path into its nine callers; the level store's \code{\verb~|instantiateList~} then has more than a hundred call sites in the image (126 in variant e3). That is measured and harmless (it is the build of section 5.1), but a much larger \code{\verb~|instantiateList~} would be copied as often.}
}
}
